+ <title>Voksigid: Orthography etc.</title>
+=20
+ <body BGCOLOR=3D"#FFFFFF">
+=20
+ <center><h3>The Orthography, Phonology, and Phonotactics of Voksigid.</=
h3></center>
+=20
+ <hr>
+=20
+ <p>
+=20
+ </p><dt>1. The alphabet consists of all the Roman letters except <b>q</=
b> and
+=20
+ <b>x.</b>
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ The letters <b>l</b> and <b>r</b> will never be used to distinguish two=
 words,
+=20
+ so that in a technical sense they will be considered the same phoneme. =
In fact,
+=20
+ however, the official sounds of <b>l</b> and <b>r</b> will be as shown =
below.
+=20
+ Because people whose native languages have only one sound similar to bo=
th, as
+=20
+ Japanese and Korean, may use that sound for both letters, we will never=
 use them
+=20
+ to distinguish minimal pairs. A similar comment holds for <b>s</b> and =
<b>z</b>;
+=20
+ Spanish, for example, has no /z/ while German tends to pronounce initia=
l
+=20
+ <b>s</b> as [z] and Italian does the same with intervocalic <b>s</b>.
+=20
+ <p>
+=20
+ </p></dt><dt>2. The sounds of the letters are as follows:
+=20
+ <p>
+=20
+ </p></dt><dd><b>a</b> (Eng. art, Fr. la)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>b</b> (Eng. boy)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>c</b> (Ital. cento =3D ch in Eng. chief, sh in Eng. shoe)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>d</b> (Eng. dog)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>e</b> (Eng. bed, Ger. Bett, beten)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>f</b> (Eng. find)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>g</b> (Eng. girl)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>h</b> (Eng. have, Ger. Bach) -- the [x] sound is allowed fo=
r the benefit
+=20
+ of such as Russian speakers who cannot pronounce [h].
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>i</b> (Eng. in, machine)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>j</b> (Eng. jam, Fr. journal)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>k</b> (Eng. key)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>l</b> (Eng. love, Ger. Liebe)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>m</b> (Eng. man)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>n</b> (Eng. sun; before <b>k</b> or <b>g,</b> as in Eng. su=
ng)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>o</b> (Brit. Eng. on, aw in Am. Eng. dawn, Ger. so)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>p</b> (Eng. pin)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>r</b> (Eng. red, Fr. rouge, Span. rojo)=20
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>s</b> (Eng. sing)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>t</b> (Eng. top)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>u</b> (Eng. push, rude)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>v</b> (Eng. very)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>w</b> (Eng. wash)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>y</b> (Eng. yet)
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><b>z</b> (Eng. zero)
+=20
+ <p>
+=20
+ </p></dd><dt>It should be noted that for the letters <b>c e h i j n o u=
,</b> sounds are
+=20
+ included that are in some languages considered distinct phonemes; this =
is
+=20
+ done to allow for ease in pronouncing by speakers of as many languages
+=20
+ as possible.
+=20
+ <p>
+=20
+ For items 3-5, the following terminology applies:
+=20
+ </p><p>
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </p></dt><dd><i>Vowel</i> =3D <b>a e i o u,</b> or any of the <i>diphth=
ongs</i> listed.
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><i>Diphthong</i> =3D <b>ai au oi.</b>=20
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><i>Pure consonant</i> =3D <b>b c d f g h j k p s t v z.</b>
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><i>Liquid</i> =3D <b>l r.</b>
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><i>Nasal</i> =3D <b>m n.</b>
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><i>Glide</i> =3D <b>w y.</b>
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><i>Semivowel</i> =3D any <i>liquid, nasal,</i> or <i>glide,</i=
> as defined
+=20
+ here.
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><i>Consonant</i> =3D any <i>pure consonant</i> or <i>semivowel=
,</i> as defined
+=20
+ here.
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><i>Voiced consonant</i> =3D <b>b d g j v z.</b>
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd><i>Unvoiced consonant</i> =3D <b>c f h k p s t.</b>
+=20
+ <p>
+=20
+ </p></dd><dt>3. A syllable consists of one to four phonemes, of the for=
m [P] [S] V [C]
+=20
+ where bracketed elements are optional, P is any pure consonant, S any
+=20
+ semivowel, V any vowel, and C any consonant, except:
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dt><dd>a. The final consonant of a syllable may not be a glide or <b>=
h</b>.
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd>b. The final consonant of one syllable, combined with the init=
ial
+=20
+ consonant(s) of an immediately following syllable in a word, may not ma=
ke any of
+=20
+ the combinations forbidden in 4.
+=20
+ <p>
+=20
+ </p></dd><dt>4. The following consonant combinations are forbidden:
+=20
+ <br>=20
+=20
+ </dt><dd>a. Voiced + unvoiced consonant or vice versa.
+=20
+ <br>
+=20
+ </dd><dd>b. Nasal consonant + nonhomoorganic stop. This specifically fo=
rbids the
+=20
+ combination of <b>m</b> + any of (<b>c d g j k t</b>) and the combinati=
on
+=20
+ of <b>n</b> with (<b>b p</b>)
+=20
+ <p>
+=20
+ </p></dd><dt>5. If a word ends in a pure consonant and the following wo=
rd does not begin
+=20
+ with a pure consonant, or if a word ends in a semivowel and the followi=
ng word=20
+=20
+ begins in a vowel, a distinct pause must be articulated at the word bou=
ndary.=20
+=20
+ In other cases, a pause <i>may</i> be made at a boundary, but need not =
be. A word=20
+=20
+ boundary is defined as anywhere where a blank space is written.
+=20
+ <p>
